          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            At its simplest, a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
            is a place where one can do calculus without worries. Let us consider the formula for a derivative:
          .maruku-equation
            %math#mathml_26ecd24cbcd4290244c987e7239084dea2eb07d3_2.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mi lim
                    %mrow
                      %mi h
                      %mo →
                      %mn 0
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo +
                      %mi h
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo −
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi h
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  f'(x) = \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{f(x + h) - f(x)}{h}
          %p
            From the fractional part of this, it appears that to be able to discuss calculus, we need to be working in a space which allows addition and scalar multiplication. However, the fact that we are considering a limit means that we only need to be able to do such things in a neighbourhood of a given point. That is, we need the concept of a
            %em local
            addition.
          %p
            Thus at its most general, a local addition at a point is simply the structure of a (piece of a) vector space near that point. This is, of course, the notion of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chart"} chart
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/atlas"} atlas
          %p
            Another direction that one could take is to ask for a system of local additions, one for each point, and ask that these fit together in some nice manner as one moves over the manifold. This leads to the notion of a
            = succeed "." do
              %strong local addition
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p The broadest definition is the following.
          #locadd.num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong local addition
              on
              %math#mathml_26ecd24cbcd4290244c987e7239084dea2eb07d3_3.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              consists of a vector bundle
              = succeed "," do
                %math#mathml_26ecd24cbcd4290244c987e7239084dea2eb07d3_4.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi π
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi E
                      %mo →
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi \colon E \to M
              = succeed "," do
                %math#mathml_26ecd24cbcd4290244c987e7239084dea2eb07d3_5.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mo ⊆
                      %mi E
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \subseteq E
              %math#mathml_26ecd24cbcd4290244c987e7239084dea2eb07d3_6.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi η
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi U
                    %mo →
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta \colon U \to M
              such that
            %ol
              %li
                The composition of
                %math#mathml_26ecd24cbcd4290244c987e7239084dea2eb07d3_7.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi η
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta
                with the zero section of
                %math#mathml_26ecd24cbcd4290244c987e7239084dea2eb07d3_8.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
                is the identity on
                = succeed "," do
                  %math#mathml_26ecd24cbcd4290244c987e7239084dea2eb07d3_9.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi M
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              %li
                there exists an open neighbourhood, say
                = succeed "," do
                  %math#mathml_26ecd24cbcd4290244c987e7239084dea2eb07d3_10.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi V
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                %math#mathml_26ecd24cbcd4290244c987e7239084dea2eb07d3_11.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                      %mo ×
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M \times M
                such that the map
                %math#mathml_26ecd24cbcd4290244c987e7239084dea2eb07d3_12.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi π
                      %mo ×
                      %mi η
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi U
                      %mo →
                      %mi M
                      %mo ×
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi \times \eta \colon U \to M \times M
                is a diffeomorphism onto
                = succeed "." do
                  %math#mathml_26ecd24cbcd4290244c987e7239084dea2eb07d3_13.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi V
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
          %h3#remarks Remarks
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                By considering the derivative of
                = succeed "," do
                  %math#mathml_26ecd24cbcd4290244c987e7239084dea2eb07d3_14.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi η
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta
                %math#mathml_26ecd24cbcd4290244c987e7239084dea2eb07d3_15.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
                must be isomorphic to
                = succeed "." do
                  %math#mathml_26ecd24cbcd4290244c987e7239084dea2eb07d3_16.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi T
                        %mi M
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T M
                %math#mathml_26ecd24cbcd4290244c987e7239084dea2eb07d3_17.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi η
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta
                naturally defines one. Nonetheless, it is common simply to take
                = succeed "." do
                  %math#mathml_26ecd24cbcd4290244c987e7239084dea2eb07d3_18.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi E
                        %mo =
                        %mi T
                        %mi M
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E = T M
            %li
              %p
                Another way to simplify the definition is to take
                = succeed "." do
                  %math#mathml_26ecd24cbcd4290244c987e7239084dea2eb07d3_19.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                        %mo =
                        %mi E
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U = E
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "#km"} KM §42.4
                %em globally defined
                local addition (though such a conjunction of “global” and “local” may grate). Given a local addition with arbitrary
                = succeed "," do
                  %math#mathml_26ecd24cbcd4290244c987e7239084dea2eb07d3_20.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                %math#mathml_26ecd24cbcd4290244c987e7239084dea2eb07d3_21.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
                onto an open subset of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math#mathml_26ecd24cbcd4290244c987e7239084dea2eb07d3_22.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            %li
              %p A useful source of local additions is from Riemannian geometry: the exponential map coming from a Riemannian structure defines a local addition.
            %li
              %p
                Another source of local additions is to apply the tubular neighbourhood theorem to the embedding of the diagonal
                = succeed "." do
                  %math#mathml_26ecd24cbcd4290244c987e7239084dea2eb07d3_23.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi M
                        %mo →
                        %mi M
                        %mo ×
                        %mi M
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M \to M \times M
          %h2#charts_from_local_additions Charts from Local Additions
          %h3#charts_for_
            Charts for
            %math#mathml_26ecd24cbcd4290244c987e7239084dea2eb07d3_24.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
          %p
            Local additions are used in constructing the manifold structure on certain mapping spaces. In brief, if
            %math#mathml_26ecd24cbcd4290244c987e7239084dea2eb07d3_25.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            is a manifold (possibly infinite dimensional) admitting a local addition, and if
            %math#mathml_26ecd24cbcd4290244c987e7239084dea2eb07d3_26.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            is a compact manifold (possibly with boundary) then
            %math#mathml_26ecd24cbcd4290244c987e7239084dea2eb07d3_27.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi N
                  %mo ,
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty(N,M)
            can be given the structure of a smooth manifold and the construction of the charts uses the local addition on
            = succeed "." do
              %math#mathml_26ecd24cbcd4290244c987e7239084dea2eb07d3_28.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#km"} KM §42
            = succeed "," do
              %math#mathml_26ecd24cbcd4290244c987e7239084dea2eb07d3_29.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                    %mo =
                    %mi pt
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N = pt
            %math#mathml_26ecd24cbcd4290244c987e7239084dea2eb07d3_30.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            itself. These charts are useful since then we have charts on both
            %math#mathml_26ecd24cbcd4290244c987e7239084dea2eb07d3_31.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            and the various mapping spaces which are all defined using the same method and so the relationships between them are that much clearer.
          #Mcharts.num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              Let
              %math#mathml_26ecd24cbcd4290244c987e7239084dea2eb07d3_32.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              be a smooth manifold,
              %math#mathml_26ecd24cbcd4290244c987e7239084dea2eb07d3_33.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi η
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi E
                    %mo ⊇
                    %mi U
                    %mo →
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta \colon E \supseteq U \to M
              a local addition on
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_26ecd24cbcd4290244c987e7239084dea2eb07d3_34.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              %math#mathml_26ecd24cbcd4290244c987e7239084dea2eb07d3_35.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mo ⊆
